## 3. End-to-end process with exact commands

Run everything from `implementation/`. Use the venv binary
(`.venv/bin/vulnpipe`) or activate the venv. For OWASP always pass both
`--config config.owasp.yaml` and `--corpus corpus/owasp.jsonl` (see
gotchas: every subcommand defaults to `corpus/juliet.jsonl`).

Shorthand used below:

```bash
V=".venv/bin/vulnpipe --config config.owasp.yaml"
C="--corpus corpus/owasp.jsonl"
F=findings/owasp
```

### 3.1 Fetch OWASP Benchmark and build the corpus

```bash
git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse \
    https://github.com/OWASP-Benchmark/BenchmarkJava.git data/owasp-benchmark
git -C data/owasp-benchmark sparse-checkout set \
    src/main/java/org/owasp/benchmark/testcode src/main/java/org/owasp/benchmark/helpers
$V corpus build --only owasp            # -> corpus/owasp.jsonl (772 rows)
```

Juliet: `.venv/bin/vulnpipe corpus build --only juliet` downloads the NIST
zip on first use and writes `corpus/juliet.jsonl` (500 method-level
snippets, balanced).

### 3.2 SAST (needs Docker)

```bash
$V sast run --tool pmd --variant both $C     # -> $F/pmd-vanilla_perSnippet.jsonl, $F/pmd-custom_perSnippet.jsonl
$V sast run --tool semgrep $C                # -> $F/semgrep_perSnippet.jsonl + $F/scan/semgrep_traces.txt
```

### 3.3 LLM as a standalone detector (needs `ollama serve` and the model pulled)

```bash
$V llm run --model deepseek-coder:6.7b-instruct --strategy zero-shot $C
$V llm run --model qwen3:8b --strategy zero-shot $C
# options: --strategy zero-shot|cot|few-shot, --limit N (smoke test),
#          --runs N --temperature T (majority vote; only if T > 0), --host URL
```

Output: `$F/<model_tag>__<strategy>_perSnippet.jsonl` plus
`$F/llm_runs/<model_tag>__<strategy>_runs.jsonl`. The model tag replaces
`:` and `/` with `_` (`qwen3:8b` -> `qwen3_8b`).

### 3.4 Pilot: choose the confirmer before the full hybrid run

```bash
.venv/bin/python scripts/pilot.py --model qwen3:8b --variants trace trace-nohints zero-shot
# --n 30 (alerts per class), --seed 7
```

Prints `TPR FPR J unparsed` per variant and appends answers to
`$F/pilot/<model_tag>__<variant>.jsonl`. See section 4.8.

### 3.5 Hybrid (needs Semgrep findings and Ollama)

```bash
SEMGREP=$F/semgrep_perSnippet.jsonl
TRACES=$F/scan/semgrep_traces.txt
# main configuration used in the thesis:
$V hybrid run --sast $SEMGREP --traces $TRACES --model qwen3:8b --scope reject $C
# variants:
$V hybrid run --sast $SEMGREP --traces $TRACES --hints --model qwen3:8b --scope reject $C
$V hybrid run --sast $SEMGREP --traces $TRACES --model qwen3:8b --scope augment $C
$V hybrid run --sast $SEMGREP --model deepseek-coder:6.7b-instruct --scope reject $C   # no traces: old prompt
```

`--sast` accepts several files (union of their alerts). Output name:
`hybrid__<sast tools joined by +>__<model_tag>__<scope>[__trace|__trace-hints]_perSnippet.jsonl`.

### 3.6 Metrics

```bash
$V metrics $C --findings $F/*_perSnippet.jsonl \
    --compare semgrep qwen3_8b__zero-shot hybrid__semgrep__qwen3_8b__reject__trace
```

Writes `metrics/owasp/per_tool.csv` and prints per-tool P/R/F1 with CI.
`--compare SAST LLM HYBRID` (tool names, not file paths) also prints HIS
and two McNemar tests (hybrid vs SAST, hybrid vs LLM).

### 3.7 Figures

```bash
$V figures $C --findings $F/*_perSnippet.jsonl --format pdf \
    --venn semgrep qwen3_8b__zero-shot hybrid__semgrep__qwen3_8b__reject__trace
# -> figures/owasp/pr_scatter.pdf, cost_vs_f1.pdf, venn_tp.pdf (needs .[figures] extra)
.venv/bin/python scripts/thesis_figures.py
# -> ../Template/Graficos/corpus_owasp.pdf, fuga_juliet.pdf, resultados_owasp.pdf, trampas_owasp.pdf (needs LaTeX: text.usetex=True)
```

### 3.8 The whole OWASP run in one script

```bash
scripts/run_owasp.sh 2>&1 | tee run_owasp.log
```

Order: corpus, PMD (both), Semgrep, DeepSeek zero-shot, DeepSeek hybrid
reject, DeepSeek hybrid augment, Qwen3 hybrid reject+trace, Qwen3 zero-shot,
Qwen3 hybrid reject+trace+hints, Qwen3 hybrid augment+trace, then
`metrics` over every `$F/*_perSnippet.jsonl`. Each `step` is skipped if its
output file exists, so rerunning after an interruption continues from the
first missing step. Timing comment in the script: Qwen3 takes about 30 s per
call, so each Qwen3 hybrid step is about 5 h and Qwen3 zero-shot about 8 h.

## 4. Module reference

### 4.1 Core: `schemas.py`, `io.py`, `config.py`, `cli.py`

- `schemas.py`
  - `Snippet(snippet_id, cwe, label, code, file, line)`: ground truth.
    `label` must be 0 (safe) or 1 (vulnerable); `__post_init__` rejects
    anything else. Constants `VULNERABLE = 1`, `SAFE = 0`.
  - `Finding(snippet_id, tool, verdict, cwe, line, rule_id, confidence,
    explanation, elapsed_ms, tokens_in, tokens_out, cost_usd,
    prompt_strategy, run_idx)`: one detector's verdict on one snippet.
    Only `snippet_id`, `tool`, `verdict` are required; `tool` is the
    detector's identity in every table.
  - `Issue`: a raw SAST alert before aggregation (several per snippet
    possible).
  - `from_dict` uses `_coerce`, which drops unknown keys, so extra columns
    in a JSONL file do not break loading.
- `io.py`: `read_jsonl`, `load_records(path, factory)`, `write_jsonl`
  (strips `None` fields, creates parent dirs, writes the whole file at once).
- `config.py`: `load(path)` returns `Config(root, raw)`. With `--config`,
  the root is the config file's directory; without it,
  `find_project_root()` walks up from the cwd looking for `config.yaml`.
  `Config.path(key)` resolves `paths.<key>`; `ensure_path` also creates it.
  Required top-level keys: `cwes, paths, corpus, metrics, llm`.
- `cli.py`: `build_parser()` defines the subcommands
  `corpus build`, `sast run`, `llm run`, `hybrid run`, `metrics`,
  `figures`; each maps to `_cmd_<name>`. `_model_ready()` is the pre-flight
  for LLM commands: checks that Ollama answers (`is_available`) and the model
  is pulled (`installed_models`), or that `OPENAI_API_KEY` is set for
  `gpt-*`/`o1`/`o3`/`o4` names.

### 4.2 Corpus: `corpus/`

- `build.py`: `build(cfg, only)` calls the dataset builder and writes
  `corpus/<name>.jsonl`. Datasets: `juliet`, `owasp`, `defects4j`.
- `owasp.py` (main corpus):
  - `read_expected(csv_path)`: reads `expectedresults-1.2.csv`, keeps
    categories `sqli` -> `"89"` and `pathtraver` -> `"22"`
    (`CATEGORY_CWE`), label from the `real vulnerability` column.
  - `build(cfg)`: one `Snippet` per test case; the unit is the whole
    servlet file (`line=1`), `snippet_id = owasp_CWE<cwe>__BenchmarkTestNNNNN`.
  - `neutralise(code)`: removes label hints before anything sees the code:
    `strip_comments`; `@WebServlet(value="/sqli-00/...")` -> `"/app/TestCase"`;
    category paths `/sqli-NN/` -> `/app/`; literals `"safe!"`, `"alsosafe"`,
    `"moresafe"` -> `"constant"`; `" SafeStuff"` -> `" SomeStuff"`;
    `"This_should_always_happen"`/`"This should never happen"` ->
    `"fixed_value"`; `BenchmarkTestNNNNN` -> `TestCase`.
- `sanitize.py`: `strip_comments(code)` removes `//` and `/* */` comments
  with a string-aware regex (a `//` inside a string literal survives) and
  drops the blank lines left behind.
- `juliet.py` (control corpus): `download`, `extract_archive`,
  `_build_prefix_map` (canonical CWE-22 maps to Juliet's CWE23 and CWE36
  directories), `iter_testcase_files`, `snippets_from_file` (`bad()` ->
  label 1, `good*()` -> label 0, method plus 20 lines of context),
  `balanced_sample` (stratified by CWE, seed 42, 250 + 250).
  Note: Juliet snippets are **not** passed through `strip_comments`.
- `javaparse.py`: `extract_methods(source)` (tree-sitter-java, with a
  brace-matching regex fallback) and `slice_with_context`.
- `defects4j.py`: stub, raises `NotImplementedError`.

### 4.3 SAST: `sast/`

- `base.py`: `SASTRunner` protocol and `aggregate_to_finding()`. The
  aggregation rule: a snippet is vulnerable iff the tool emits at least one
  issue in it whose rule maps to a target CWE (issues with no CWE count as
  relevant). The first relevant issue supplies `rule_id`, `line`,
  `explanation`.
- `container.py`: `ensure_docker()`, `run_container(image, args, mounts)`
  (`docker run --rm -v host:container ...`, returns rc, stdout, stderr,
  elapsed ms), `parse_sarif(path, tool, rule_cwe, only_mapped=True)`
  (SARIF -> `Issue`s; the snippet id is the artifact file name without
  `.java`; unmapped rules are dropped).
- `materialize.py`: `materialize(snippets, scan_dir, resolve_source)`
  deletes old `*.java` in `scan_dir` and writes `<snippet_id>.java` per
  snippet. `wrap_snippet()`: a snippet that is already a full compilation
  unit (OWASP: has `package`/`import` and balanced braces) is written as is;
  a Juliet method slice is re-extracted from the original file
  (`build_source_index`, `_brace_complete_method`) and wrapped as
  `final class S { <method> }` so PMD can parse it.
- `pmd.py`: `VARIANTS` holds `pmd-vanilla` (built-in
  `category/java/security.xml`, empty rule->CWE map) and `pmd-custom`
  (`/rules/pmd_cwe89_cwe22.xml`, rules `SqlInjectionConcatExec` -> 89,
  `PathTraversalFileSink` -> 22). `run_variant()` materializes, runs
  `pmd check -f sarif`, accepts exit codes 0/4/5, parses SARIF, and
  `aggregate_findings()` emits one `Finding` per corpus snippet (no issue ->
  safe). `elapsed_ms` is the total batch time copied to every row.
- `semgrep.py`: `RULE_CWE` maps the SARIF rule ids `rules.sqli-taint-jdbc`
  -> 89 and `rules.path-traversal-taint-file` -> 22 (Semgrep prefixes ids
  with `rules.`). `run_semgrep()` does two container runs over the same
  directory:
  1. `semgrep scan --config ... --sarif --output /src/semgrep.sarif` -> the
     verdicts.
  2. the same scan in text mode with `--dataflow-traces
     --max-lines-per-finding 50 --max-chars-per-line 400`; stdout is saved
     to `scan/semgrep_traces.txt`. Semgrep OSS does not include traces in
     JSON or SARIF, which is why this second pass exists.

  `parse_text_traces(text)` reads that text file and returns
  `{snippet_id: {"source": [lines], "steps": [lines], "sink": [lines]}}`.
  It tracks the current file (`/src/<id>.java`), switches mode on the
  markers "Taint comes from" / "intermediate variables" / "reaches the
  sink", and collects the numbers before `┆`. Only line numbers are kept;
  the prompt re-reads the code.

Rulesets:
- `semgrep_cwe89_cwe22.yml`: `mode: taint`. Sources: request parameters,
  headers, cookies, query string, `getTheParameter`/`getTheCookie` (OWASP
  helpers), `System.getenv`, `getProperty`, readers, `$A[$I]`. Sanitizers
  for SQLi: `Integer.parseInt`, `Long.parseLong`, alphanumeric
  `replaceAll`, `encodeForSQL`; for path traversal: `getCanonicalPath`,
  `normalize`, `replace("..", ...)`. Sinks: JDBC/Spring `execute*`,
  `prepareStatement`, `query*`, `update`; `new File(...)`, file streams,
  `RandomAccessFile`, `Paths.get`.
- `pmd_cwe89_cwe22.xml`: same sink set, but purely syntactic: flags a sink
  whose argument list has no string literal. No dataflow, so it
  over-approximates (on OWASP: recall 0.97, FPR 0.97).

### 4.4 LLM: `llm/`

- `base.py`: `Verdict` dataclass, `LLMBackend` protocol,
  `_first_json_object(text)` (first balanced `{...}` that parses), and
  `parse_verdict(text)`: reads key `verdict`, `decision`, `label` or
  `answer`; maps words (`vulnerable/yes/true/confirm/1` vs
  `safe/no/false/reject/0/none`); a JSON object with no usable key -> safe;
  no JSON at all -> keyword search over the whole text (any vulnerable word
  -> vulnerable, else safe).
- `prompts.py`:
  - `zero_shot(code)`: system line ("You are a Java security auditor...
    CWE-89 or CWE-22") + JSON spec `{verdict, cwe, line, reason}` + code.
  - `cot(code)`: same plus "Reason step by step"; run without JSON mode.
  - `few_shot(code, examples)`: shots prepended.
  - `static_augmented(code, tool, cwe, line)`: the original hybrid prompt:
    "<tool> flagged a possible CWE-x at line N. Confirm or reject",
    answer `{decision: confirm|reject, reason}`.
  - `trace_confirm(code, cwe, trace, hints=True)`: the current hybrid
    prompt. Code is line-numbered; the Semgrep path is shown as Source /
    Through / Sink lines; the model is told taint analyzers report many
    false alarms and to follow the value that actually reaches the sink.
    With `hints=True` it adds: "it may be replaced by a constant, chosen by
    a condition whose outcome is fixed, sanitized, or taken from a different
    element than the tainted one". Answer `{analysis, verdict}` with
    `analysis` first so the model reasons before committing.
  - `BUILDERS` maps strategy name -> builder.
- `ollama.py`: `OllamaBackend(model, host, temperature=0.0, num_predict=512,
  timeout=300)`. `generate(prompt, force_json=True)` POSTs to
  `/api/generate` with `stream: false`. For models starting with
  `REASONING_PREFIXES = ("qwen3", "gpt-oss", "deepseek-r1")` it sets
  `think: true`, `num_predict=4096`, `num_ctx=8192` and a 900 s timeout
  (thinking is returned separately and not stored). `force_json` sets
  `format: "json"`. On HTTP 5xx it retries once without `format` (Ollama's
  JSON grammar sometimes fails with a bare 500). Returns `GenResult(text,
  tokens_in=prompt_eval_count, tokens_out=eval_count,
  elapsed_ms=total_duration)`. Also `is_available()`, `installed_models()`.
- `openai.py`: `OpenAIBackend` (Chat Completions over urllib) and
  `backend_for(model, host, temperature)`. No OpenAI model has been run,
  but this module **is** on the active path: `backend_for` is the
  dispatcher used by `llm/run.py`, `hybrid/run.py` and `scripts/pilot.py`;
  for any non-`gpt-*`/`o*` name it just returns an `OllamaBackend`.
- `run.py`: `run(cfg, corpus, model, strategy, runs, temperature, limit,
  host)`. For each snippet: build prompt (`_build_prompt`), call the model
  `n_runs` times (`n_runs` is forced to 1 when temperature is 0), parse,
  vote (`_vote`: vulnerable if at least half the runs say vulnerable, so a
  tie counts as vulnerable), and write one per-snippet `Finding` (tokens and
  time summed across runs) plus one row per call into `llm_runs/`.
  Few-shot uses the first vulnerable and first safe snippet of the corpus
  as shots and removes them from the evaluation set (`_held_out`).
- `mock.py`: `MockBackend`, a keyword heuristic used only by tests.

### 4.5 Hybrid: `hybrid/run.py`

- `sast_candidates(findings)`: union of all input SAST findings with
  `verdict == 1`; the first finding seen per snippet is the representative
  (gives `tool`, `cwe`, `line`, `rule_id`).
- `run(cfg, corpus, sast_findings, model, scope, runs, temperature, limit,
  host, traces, hints)`. Per snippet:
  - **candidate**: if a trace exists for it, prompt = `trace_confirm(code,
    cwe, trace, hints)` and `prompt_strategy="trace-confirm"`; otherwise
    `static_augmented(...)`. Verdict from `_confirm_vote` (strict majority,
    a tie rejects). Confirmed rows keep the alert's `cwe` and `line`.
  - **not a candidate, scope `reject`**: verdict 0, no LLM call
    (`explanation="no SAST candidate"`). Recall can never exceed the SAST
    input's recall.
  - **not a candidate, scope `augment`**: a `zero_shot` call, so the
    hybrid can also find what SAST missed (at the cost of 772 calls instead
    of about 632).
- `_tool_name()` builds the tool string; with traces it appends `__trace`
  or `__trace-hints`.
- The CWE sent to the prompt is the alert's CWE, falling back to the
  snippet's CWE.

### 4.6 Metrics: `metrics/`

- `join.py`: `join_to_truth` (drops findings for unknown snippets),
  `confusion_by_tool` (a snippet with no row for a tool counts as a
  negative verdict), `aligned_predictions` (paired `y_true` and `y_pred`
  arrays in corpus order, used by bootstrap and McNemar).
- `compute.py`: `metrics_from_confusion` (precision, recall, FPR, F1),
  `his`, `bootstrap_f1_ci`, `mcnemar`. Details in section 6.

### 4.7 Figures: `figures/plots.py` and `scripts/thesis_figures.py`

- `figure_data(findings, corpus)` returns one `FigureRow` per tool
  (metrics, mean `tokens_out`, mean `elapsed_ms`, set of TP snippet ids).
- `pr_scatter`, `cost_vs_f1` (SAST is pinned at x = 0 tokens), `venn_tp`
  (needs `matplotlib-venn`), `make_all`. Labels are made by `_short()`.
- `scripts/thesis_figures.py`: `corpus_composition()` (stacked bars from
  `corpus/owasp.jsonl`) and `leakage()` (the Juliet leakage control
  experiment). The leakage numbers are **hard-coded** in the script
  (DeepSeek zero-shot on 74 Juliet cases: Original / Sin pistas / Solo el
  método); no code in the repo regenerates them. `main_results()` draws
  precision, recall, F1 and FPR for Semgrep, Qwen3-8B zero-shot and the
  trace hybrid, and `trap_patterns()` splits Semgrep's false alerts by
  benchmark trap (arithmetic condition, `switch`, collection, matched by
  regex on the code) into those the confirmer discarded or kept.

### 4.8 Pilot: `scripts/pilot.py`

Purpose: a cheap check that a confirmer's answer depends on the code before
running it on all 632 alerts. It loads `corpus/owasp.jsonl`, the Semgrep
alerts (`verdict == 1` in `semgrep_perSnippet.jsonl`) and the parsed
traces, then samples 30 true alerts (label 1) and 30 false alerts (label 0)
with `random.Random(7)`. For each variant it asks the model once per alert:

| variant | prompt |
|---|---|
| `trace` | `trace_confirm(..., hints=True)` (the default) |
| `trace-nohints` | `trace_confirm(..., hints=False)` |
| `confirm` | `static_augmented(...)` (the prompt of the first DeepSeek hybrid run) |
| `zero-shot` | `zero_shot(code)` |

It reports TPR (share of true alerts kept), FPR (share of false alerts
kept) and Youden J = TPR - FPR. J near 0 means the model says the same
thing regardless of the code. It is resumable row by row (appends and
flushes after each answer). Note that pilot variant `trace` corresponds to
`hybrid run --traces --hints`, and `trace-nohints` to `hybrid run --traces`
without `--hints`.

## 5. Data formats

All files are JSONL (one JSON object per line), `None` fields omitted.

**Corpus** (`corpus/owasp.jsonl`, 772 rows):

```json
{"snippet_id": "owasp_CWE22__BenchmarkTest00001", "cwe": "22", "label": 1,
 "code": "package org.owasp.benchmark.testcode;\nimport java.io.IOException;\n...@WebServlet(value = \"/app/TestCase\")\npublic class TestCase extends HttpServlet {...",
 "file": "BenchmarkTest00001.java", "line": 1}
```

